<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kodava people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kodavas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kodavas are a people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodagu">Kodagu</a> (Coorg) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, who speak the Dravidian Kodava language. Traditionally landholding farmers and warriors organised in patrilineal clans, they served in the armies of the rajas of Coorg and later in large numbers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>. They are known for their ancestor worship and distinctive festivals and dress.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kodava-people.jpg" alt="Kodava people" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P M Muthanna · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kathiyath_by_Kodava_Youth-26-10-2013-P_M_Muthanna_(6).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The names Kodava, for the people, language and culture, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodagu">Kodagu</a>, for the land, share the root koda, which some take to mean mist, others hills or west, all fitting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>. In their own language the land is Kodava Naad; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a> anglicised the people's name as Coorgs. Works of the Sangam period place parts of Kodagu in Karkanadu, the hilly eastern district of the Ezhimala dynasty.</p><p>The region later passed under the Kadamba and the Gangas, briefly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and the Vijaynagara rulers. From around 1600 the Haleri Rajas ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodagu">Kodagu</a>, and under them the Kodavas began to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> festivals such as Ugadi, Ayudha Puja and Mahashivaratri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1834 the last Haleri Raja, Chikka Vira Raja, fell out with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, which invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodagu">Kodagu</a>. The campaign was short but bloody; resistance was fiercest near Somwarpet, where the Coorgs were led by Mathanda Appachu, but it ended when the Raja sent his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">Diwan</a>, Apparanda Bopu, to surrender and lead the British from Kushalnagar into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madikeri">Madikeri</a>. Kodagu was annexed, the Raja exiled, and large numbers of European planters settled in its forests to grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, which changed the economy and land management of Kodava society. Kodavas later fought in both World Wars and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Kodavas live in okka family groups scattered over farms and forest holdings, with the ancestral ainmane houses as their meeting points; most towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodagu">Kodagu</a> are recent and largely home to non-Kodavas. Under the Jamma tenure instituted by the Rajas, lands were held almost wholly by Kodavas as a hereditary right, indivisible and inalienable, together with the adjoining woods. Each village keeps at least one devarakadu, a sacred grove believed to be an abode of the gods, where poaching and felling are forbidden.</p><p>Their festivals follow the farming year. Kail Poldh, the festival of arms on 3 September, marks the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> transplanting, when the weapons kept in the prayer room were taken out to guard the crops from wild boar. At Kaveri Sankramana in mid-October pilgrims gather at Talakaveri, the source of the Kaveri, and carry its holy water home, and Puttari, the festival of new rice, falls on a full-moon night in late November or early December. Because of their militia duties, the Indian Arms Act of 1878 exempted all persons of Kodava race from its restrictions on firearms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kodava people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Rajas ruled the warrior <b>Kodava</b> landholders through their own officials.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-coorg">Kingdom of Coorg</a></li></ul></section>
